    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Kumasi was founded around 1680 as the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Ashanti Empire</a>, and its ruler, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asantehene">Asantehene</a>, was also ruler of the empire.</li><li>The Aowin sacked the city in 1718 and 1719, and the British burnt parts of it in 1874, invaded it again in 1895 and 1896 and ended the war of 1900 with the defeat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Ashanti</a> by 1 January 1901.</li><li>Under British rule from 1901 the city was modernised with railways, a fort, hospitals and colleges, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>'s independence in 1957 it became the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Ashanti</a> Region.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>According to legend, it was at Kumasi that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okomfo-anokye">Okomfo Anokye</a> received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-stool">Golden Stool</a>, the embodiment of the soul of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Ashanti</a> nation, and the city's name means &quot;under the kum tree&quot;.</p></aside>
    </section>

