    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1680</span><span class="rf-text">Osei Kofi Tutu I founds Kumasi as the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Ashanti Empire</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1695</span><span class="rf-text">Kumasi rises to prominence as the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Ashanti Empire</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1718</span><span class="rf-text">The Aowin king Ebirimoro invades and sacks Kumasi, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opoku-ware-i">Opoku Ware I</a> beats back the invasion</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1874</span><span class="rf-text">The British burn parts of the city, including the royal residence, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-anglo-ashanti-war">Third Anglo-Ashanti War</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1896</span><span class="rf-text">The British invade Kumasi again and build Fort Kumasi at the centre of the town</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1900</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Ashanti</a> besiege <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederic-hodgson">Governor Hodgson</a> in the fort until he breaks out on 23 June</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1901</span><span class="rf-text">The British formally annex Kumasi and its population has fallen to 3000</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1957</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a> becomes independent and Kumasi becomes the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Ashanti</a> Region</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Kumasi stands in a rain forest region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a> near Lake Bosomtwe, about 200 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/accra">Accra</a>. The city was built over the eastern slopes of a ridge, rising from the marshes of the Nsuben rivers. Its name comes from the Twi word Kumase, meaning "under the kum tree", and the word was the name of an Okum tree in Kwaman, planted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okomfo-anokye">Okomfo Anokye</a>.</p><p>Kumasi was founded around 1680, or in the 1680s, by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asantehene">Asantehene</a> Osei Kofi Tutu I as the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Ashanti Empire</a>. Accounts of its formation differ. One holds that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osei-tutu-i">Osei Tutu</a> negotiated for the land under a kum tree. Other oral sources say that Nana Oti Akenten negotiated with the chief of Tafo for a plot of land under a kum tree. Other traditions hold that Oti built Kwaman and that his son Nana Obiri Yeboa created Kumasi instead. The majority of oral sources attribute the choice of site to Okomfo Anokye, who was said to have planted two kum seeds, one in Kwaman and one in Kumawu, and to have decreed that the one that grew would be the capital of Osei Tutu's empire.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The city rose to prominence in 1695, when it became the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Ashanti Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osei-tutu-i">Osei Tutu</a>. After the victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denkyira">Denkyira</a> in 1701 the Ashanti empire became the primary state among the Ashanti. Visitors of the 19th century described a large and orderly town. In 1816 Huydecooper wrote of clean and straight streets and houses excellently built, most of them of one storey. In 1817 Thomas Edward Bowdich found that the central city, without suburbs such as Bantama and Asafo, was oblong and about 4 miles round, with four principal streets half a mile long and 50 to 100 yards wide. It had 27 streets then, and another source named 50 by the mid-1880s.</p><p>Estimates of the population under the empire vary. In the early 19th century Ashanti sources gave 100,000 and European sources 12,000 to 15,000, and another source of 1820 gave more than 200,000. According to the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivor-wilks">Ivor Wilks</a> the city may have had 40,000 people in the 1860s. Before 1883 there were 150 loggias called dampans in the city. The Great Market, Dwaberem, served as a marketplace, parade ground, royal courthouse and place to receive prominent visitors, and Apremoso, named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a> captured at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-feyiase">Battle of Feyiase</a>, was where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asantehene">Asantehene</a>, officials and people could gather.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/prempeh-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prempeh-i.jpg" alt="Prempeh I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/prempeh-i">Prempeh I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Prempeh_of_Ashanti_and_followers_(Political_Prisoners)_Mah%C3%A9,_Seychelles.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The ruler of Kumasi, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asantehene">Asantehene</a>, was also ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Ashanti Empire</a>, which at its peak covered large parts of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-coast">Ivory Coast</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osei-tutu-i">Osei Tutu</a> made it the capital, and in 1718 and 1719 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opoku-ware-i">Opoku Ware I</a> defended it against the Aowin. The palace complex stood in the eastern quarter of the central city.</p><p>The British took parts of the city in 1874 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-anglo-ashanti-war">Third Anglo-Ashanti War</a> and invaded it again between 1895 and 1896. The state of Ashanti then became a formal colony of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>, and Kumasi was formally annexed in 1901. The Kumasi Public Health Board was established in 1925 as the modern local government. In 1926 the Ashanti king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prempeh-i">Prempeh I</a> returned after 30 years of exile in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seychelles">Seychelles</a>, and Kumasi was given ceremonial control over the Ashanti sub-states. The colonial administration restored the full role of king in 1935.</p><p>After Ghana gained independence in 1957 Kumasi became the capital of the Ashanti Region. It remains the seat of the Asantehene. In 1988 it was designated a Metropolitan under PNDCL 208, and the Local Government Act of 1993 allowed local assembly representatives to be elected and to take part in planning.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The city had 77 wards, and specific wards were inhabited by a particular class of artisans or workers: goldsmiths, umbrella makers, coffin makers, executioners and city patrollers each lived in their own sections. Bowdich recorded that each street was named and that a superior captain was placed in charge of it. A uniformed police maintained security. He also recorded public toilets on the outskirts of the town and toilets in each house, into which boiling water was poured daily, and the waste of the houses was burned behind the street every morning. In peaceful times the red and white clay of the buildings was regularly polished and renewed.</p><p>Muslims from Islamic states in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a> formed a permanent settlement in Kumasi during the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osei-bonsu">Osei Bonsu</a>. Dupuis recorded about 1000 of them in 1820, and the first Islamic schools of the empire were founded in Kumasi in the early 19th century. Kumasi is the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Asante</a> culture, and it is known for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kente-cloth">kente</a>, a traditional textile.</p><p>Under British rule the economy became cash-based, with industries for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> and cocoa, and 11 European firms were established in 1907. The colonial government removed barriers to migration, and people from the Northern Territories, Syrians, Lebanese and Indians came. Schmidt argues that the colonial government segregated Kumasi's residential districts on racial lines.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/golden-stool"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/golden-stool.jpg" alt="Golden Stool" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/golden-stool">Golden Stool</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Golden_stool_31_January_1935.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">UK gov, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 1718 and 1719 the Aowin king Ebirimoro invaded Kumasi and sacked the capital while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asantehene">Asantehene</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opoku-ware-i">Opoku Ware I</a> and his army were away at the front. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axim">Axim</a> recorded that the Aowin plundered the city's gold and captured 20,000 of its people, and a number of the city's royals were killed. Opoku Ware was able to beat back the invasion.</p><p>In 1874, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-anglo-ashanti-war">Third Anglo-Ashanti War</a>, the British burnt parts of the city, including the royal residence. Native carriers were generally blamed for torching it, and British engineers made strenuous efforts to put out the fires. A civil war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Asante</a> lasting five years, which ended by 1888, caused further decline. R. Austin Freeman, visiting in 1888, found a large clearing in the forest with scattered groups of houses and wasteland where houses had stood, and wrote that since the destruction of 1874 the people did not seem to have had heart to rebuild them.</p><p>Between 1895 and 1896 the British invaded Kumasi again. The Secretary of State for the Colonies justified the expedition by saying it was to appoint a resident in Kumasi who would see that the king carried out his engagements. In 1900 a war broke out after Governor Frederick Mitchell Hodgson made a speech in Kumasi demanding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-stool">Golden Stool</a>. The Ashanti held Hodgson under siege in the fort until he broke out of the city on 23 June 1900, and British war operations ended on 1 January 1901 with the defeat of the Ashanti. By 1901 the population had declined to 3000.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/prempeh-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prempeh-ii.jpg" alt="Prempeh II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/prempeh-ii">Prempeh II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PrempehII.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Kumasi holds an important place in the history of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Ashanti</a> people. Legend claims that it was here that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okomfo-anokye">Okomfo Anokye</a> received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-stool">Golden Stool</a>, the embodiment of the soul of the Ashanti nation. Royal Asante sites in the city include the Manhyia Palace, dating from 1925 and now a museum, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asantehene">Asantehene</a>'s Palace, built in 1972, the Okomfo Anokye Sword, and the Centre for National Culture, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prempeh-ii">Prempeh II</a> Jubilee Museum holds Asante regalia and a reproduction of the Golden Stool. Fort Kumasi, built in 1896 to replace an Asante fort, is now a museum.</p><p>The British modernised the city from 1896. The wetlands near the centre were drained in 1905 for the railway linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/accra">Accra</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-coast">Cape Coast</a>, pipe-borne water was developed in the 1920s, and the Central Market was completed in 1925. The University of Science and Technology was established in 1952, and in 1953 Kumasi hosted the Conference of West African Nationalists. In 1945 Maxwell Fry and Jane Drew designed the city's first development plan on the Garden City model of Howard, with green belts, and in the middle of the 20th century Kumasi was called the Garden City of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>.</p><p>Kumasi is now the second largest city in Ghana, with a population of about 444,000. Its Kejetia Market is the largest in West Africa.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Kumasi, in the forest of Ghana, was founded around 1680 as the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Ashanti Empire</a>. The Aowin sacked it in 1718 and 1719, the British burnt parts of it in 1874 and invaded it again in 1895 and 1896, and it was annexed in 1901. It is now the capital of the Ashanti Region.</em></p>

